{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FtN4Ru78YR1U5QgE2hGrbj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Abandon convergence as headline. Reframe paper around the robust finding that survives all checks:\n  per-example divergence (Jaccard <0.10) is architecture-intrinsic and persists regardless of aggregate\n  convergence trends. New title direction: 'Aggregate Metrics Hide Architecture-Specific Behavior in\n  Hybrid Language Models.' Lead claim becomes: hybrid architectures that look identical on benchmarks\n  are solving fundamentally different subsets of examples — standard evaluation is blind to this.\n  Convergence (when it exists) is demoted to a secondary observation. Practical implication reframed:\n  architecture selection matters even when leaderboard scores converge, because failure modes don't\n  overlap → ensemble/routing potential. Trade-off: weaker theoretical story, but more defensible claims.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Re-run lm-eval with --log_samples on 1.3B models for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge.\n      Collect per-example correctness vectors across architectures and attention ratios.\n      Compute pairwise Jaccard on helped/hurt sets; test whether per-example convergence\n      is absent on non-LAMBADA benchmarks.\n    expected_results:\n      hellaswag_jaccard_mean: 0.08\n      hellaswag_jaccard_std: 0.03\n      arc_easy_jaccard_mean: 0.11\n      arc_challenge_jaccard_mean: 0.13\n      hellaswag_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.15\n      hellaswag_per_example_convergence_p: 0.62\n      arc_easy_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.40\n      arc_easy_per_example_convergence_p: 0.35\n      winogrande_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.20\n      winogrande_per_example_convergence_p: 0.55\n      n_benchmarks_with_per_example_convergence: 0\n      arc_easy_aggregate_convergence_suggestive: true\n      arc_easy_per_example_mechanism_matches_lambada: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, per-example analysis on HellaSwag and WinoGrande reveals NO convergence\n      signal at sample level either — confirming task-specificity holds. However, ARC-Easy shows\n      a weak but non-trivial per-example signal (rho=-0.40, p=0.35) that partially mirrors LAMBADA's\n      pattern, threatening the clean \"retrieval-specific\" story. The effect is too weak to be\n      significant but too present to ignore, collapsing the task-specificity claim into a muddier\n      \"convergence is generic but weak everywhere except LAMBADA where GDN amplifies it\" narrative.\n      Jaccard values on HellaSwag (~0.08) confirm per-example divergence generalizes across benchmarks\n      — this is the one robust finding. ARC tasks show slightly higher Jaccard (0.11-0.13), suggesting\n      reasoning tasks have marginally more overlap in architecture behavior but still fundamentally\n      low. The critical implication: convergence is NOT cleanly retrieval-specific (undermining Claim 1's\n      dose-response framing), but per-example divergence IS universal (supporting the reframed narrative).\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"Aggregate ρ (std vs attn)\", \"Per-example ρ\", \"Per-example p\", \"Mean Jaccard\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"-1.00\", \"-0.90\", \"0.017\", \"0.07\", \"Convergent (but GDN-driven)\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"-0.70\", \"-0.40\", \"0.350\", \"0.11\", \"Weak signal, non-significant\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"-0.30\", \"-0.10\", \"0.780\", \"0.13\", \"No convergence\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.30\", \"-0.15\", \"0.620\", \"0.08\", \"No convergence\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.10\", \"-0.20\", \"0.550\", \"0.09\", \"No convergence\"]\n      - title: \"Pairwise Jaccard similarity on helped sets (1.3B, h3-1 config)\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"GDN-RetNet\", \"GDN-HGRN2\", \"RetNet-HGRN2\", \"Mean\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"0.06\", \"0.08\", \"0.07\", \"0.07\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"0.10\", \"0.12\", \"0.11\", \"0.11\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"0.11\", \"0.14\", \"0.13\", \"0.13\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.07\", \"0.09\", \"0.08\", \"0.08\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.08\", \"0.10\", \"0.09\", \"0.09\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap of Jaccard similarity across all benchmark × architecture pairs. Uniformly cold (blue) across all benchmarks, demonstrating that per-example divergence is benchmark-agnostic. LAMBADA and HellaSwag nearly identical in Jaccard despite opposite aggregate convergence trends.\"\n      - \"Scatter: aggregate convergence ρ vs per-example convergence ρ per benchmark. No clean linear relationship; ARC-Easy is an outlier with moderate per-example signal despite aggregate suggestiveness, breaking the expected dose-response pattern.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ARC-Easy per-example signal (ρ=-0.40) is the worst-case complication. It's not significant\n      (p=0.35) but it's not zero either. A reviewer can argue: \"convergence exists weakly everywhere,\n      LAMBADA just happens to cross significance because GDN's anomalous behavior inflates the signal.\"\n      This undermines the retrieval-specificity framing. However, the universal low Jaccard (all <0.15)\n      is the paper's salvation — it doesn't depend on convergence at all. The reframe: \"regardless of\n      whether architectures converge in aggregate, they solve different examples\" is supported by every\n      benchmark. Dose-response narrative for convergence strength is dead; replace with: \"per-example\n      divergence is the invariant, convergence is the variable.\"\n\n  - name: \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Extend Phase 1 surface feature analysis with deeper features: (a) perplexity of target token\n      under pure recurrent baseline (no attention), (b) topic/domain classification of LAMBADA\n      contexts using a lightweight classifier or embedding clustering. Test whether these features\n      differentiate helped vs hurt samples within and across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      perplexity_gdn_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.18\n      perplexity_hgrn2_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.09\n      perplexity_retnet_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.31\n      perplexity_cross_arch_interaction_p: 0.42\n      topic_gdn_chi2_p: 0.35\n      topic_hgrn2_chi2_p: 0.22\n      topic_retnet_chi2_p: 0.47\n      topic_cross_arch_difference_p: 0.61\n      any_feature_significant_p_lt_01: false\n      n_features_marginal_p_lt_05: 1\n      best_single_feature_auc: 0.54\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, ALL Phase 2 features are non-significant. Perplexity under baseline\n      shows a marginal trend for HGRN2 (p=0.09) — helped samples tend to have slightly higher\n      baseline perplexity, suggesting attention helps on \"harder\" items — but the effect doesn't\n      replicate across GDN (p=0.18) or RetNet (p=0.31), and the cross-architecture interaction\n      is nowhere near significant (p=0.42). Topic/domain analysis reveals no systematic pattern\n      at all (all p>0.2). The best single-feature AUC for predicting helped/hurt is 0.54,\n      barely above chance. This means we have ZERO mechanistic toehold: we cannot explain what\n      makes a sample helped vs hurt by any measurable property. The architecture-specific effect\n      is real (Jaccard proves it) but opaque. This is the worst case for Concern C — no causal\n      mechanism is articulable. The reframe must embrace this opacity: \"the effect is detectable\n      but not yet explainable, which itself is a finding about the limits of current interpretability.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Phase 2 feature analysis: helped vs hurt samples (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\"\n        columns: [\"Feature\", \"GDN p-value\", \"HGRN2 p-value\", \"RetNet p-value\", \"Cross-arch interaction p\", \"Best AUC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Baseline perplexity\", \"0.18\", \"0.09\", \"0.31\", \"0.42\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"Topic (fiction/non-fiction)\", \"0.35\", \"0.22\", \"0.47\", \"0.61\", \"0.51\"]\n          - [\"Topic (10-cluster)\", \"0.41\", \"0.29\", \"0.53\", \"0.58\", \"0.52\"]\n          - [\"Token frequency (Phase 1)\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \">0.5\", \"0.51\"]\n          - [\"Context length (Phase 1)\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \"0.012\", \">0.5\", \"0.52\"]\n      - title: \"Combined feature model (logistic regression, helped vs hurt)\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"All features AUC\", \"95% CI\", \"Permutation baseline AUC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN\", \"0.53\", \"[0.49, 0.57]\", \"0.50\"]\n          - [\"HGRN2\", \"0.55\", \"[0.51, 0.59]\", \"0.50\"]\n          - [\"RetNet\", \"0.52\", \"[0.48, 0.56]\", \"0.50\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Violin plots of baseline perplexity for helped vs hurt samples, split by architecture. Distributions almost entirely overlapping. HGRN2 shows a slight rightward shift for helped samples but the overlap is >90%.\"\n      - \"ROC curves for combined feature model per architecture. All curves hug the diagonal, with AUCs in [0.52, 0.55]. Visual confirmation that no accessible feature set predicts helped/hurt status.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Complete mechanistic failure. Six features tested across two phases, none reaches significance\n      with cross-architecture consistency. The HGRN2 perplexity trend (p=0.09) is tantalizing but\n      unreliable — it doesn't survive multiple comparison correction and doesn't generalize to the\n      other architectures. The combined model AUC of 0.53-0.55 means that even throwing all features\n      together, we predict helped/hurt status barely better than a coin flip. This is simultaneously\n      the worst and most interesting outcome: the architecture-specific effect is NOT driven by\n      any obvious data property. For the reframed paper, this becomes: \"standard sample features\n      cannot explain which examples each architecture handles differently, suggesting the divergence\n      operates at a representational level not captured by surface or moderate-depth features.\"\n\n  - name: \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Extract attention weight matrices from h3-1 hybrid models for all 3 architectures on LAMBADA\n      samples. Analyze: position-level attention entropy, correlation between attention focus and\n      helped/hurt identity, cross-architecture attention pattern similarity (CKA or cosine).\n    expected_results:\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_gdn: 0.07\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_hgrn2: 0.12\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_retnet: 0.04\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_retnet: 0.31\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_hgrn2: 0.28\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_retnet_hgrn2: 0.35\n      attention_focus_position_correlation_with_effect: 0.09\n      attention_pattern_predicts_helped_auc: 0.56\n      interpretable_pattern_found: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, attention patterns show low correlation with helped/hurt identity (r<0.15\n      for all architectures). Attention entropy — a measure of how focused vs diffuse the attention\n      is — differs only trivially between helped and hurt samples. Cross-architecture CKA similarity\n      is moderate (~0.30), meaning attention layers are doing somewhat similar things structurally\n      across architectures, but this structural similarity does NOT predict the divergent per-example\n      outcomes. The attention pattern AUC for predicting helped/hurt (0.56) is only marginally\n      better than the feature-based model and far from interpretable. No clear \"smoking gun\" pattern\n      emerges (e.g., no finding like \"GDN attends to nearby context while RetNet attends to distant\n      context on helped samples\"). This makes Concern C (no causal mechanism) essentially unanswerable\n      within the scope of this paper. The attention layer is doing something architecture-specific,\n      but what it's doing doesn't cleanly map onto which examples benefit.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Attention pattern analysis (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"GDN\", \"HGRN2\", \"RetNet\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Mean attention entropy (helped)\", \"2.31\", \"2.45\", \"2.28\"]\n          - [\"Mean attention entropy (hurt)\", \"2.34\", \"2.39\", \"2.30\"]\n          - [\"Entropy correlation with effect size\", \"0.07\", \"0.12\", \"0.04\"]\n          - [\"% attention on last 10 tokens (helped)\", \"0.34\", \"0.29\", \"0.36\"]\n          - [\"% attention on last 10 tokens (hurt)\", \"0.33\", \"0.30\", \"0.35\"]\n          - [\"Attention predicts helped AUC\", \"0.54\", \"0.57\", \"0.53\"]\n      - title: \"Cross-architecture attention similarity (CKA, h3-1 layer)\"\n        columns: [\"Pair\", \"CKA (all samples)\", \"CKA (helped subset)\", \"CKA (hurt subset)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN–RetNet\", \"0.31\", \"0.29\", \"0.33\"]\n          - [\"GDN–HGRN2\", \"0.28\", \"0.26\", \"0.30\"]\n          - [\"RetNet–HGRN2\", \"0.35\", \"0.33\", \"0.37\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Attention heatmaps for 3 representative LAMBADA examples (one helped by all, one hurt by all, one divergent). No visually distinguishable pattern difference. Attention distributions look qualitatively similar across architectures even on divergent samples.\"\n      - \"Scatter plot: per-sample attention entropy vs effect size (log-prob change), colored by architecture. Cloud is roughly circular with no discernible trend. Correlation lines nearly flat (all |r|<0.15).\"\n    analysis: >\n      Attention analysis provides no mechanistic explanation. The moderate CKA values (~0.30) are\n      actually a nuisance finding — attention is somewhat similar across architectures, which means\n      the divergent per-example outcomes must arise from the INTERACTION between attention and the\n      different recurrence mechanisms, not from attention alone. This is plausible but untestable\n      within our experimental scope — we'd need to ablate the recurrence mechanism while holding\n      attention fixed, which requires retraining. For the reframed paper, this section becomes:\n      \"attention patterns alone do not explain architecture-specific effects, suggesting the mechanism\n      operates through attention-recurrence interaction\" — a honest null result that points to\n      future work. The figures can still appear in the paper as evidence of thoroughness.\n\n  - name: \"Architecture expansion check\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Search Wang et al. model zoo and public repositories for additional hybrid architectures\n      (Mamba-2 hybrids, RWKV hybrids, Griffin, etc.) compatible with lm-eval. If found, evaluate\n      on LAMBADA to test convergence generalization.\n    expected_results:\n      additional_architectures_found: 0\n      additional_architectures_compatible: 0\n      n_architectures_tested: 3\n      wang_et_al_only_gdn_retnet_hgrn2: true\n      public_hybrid_checkpoints_available: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, no additional architectures are available. Wang et al.'s model zoo contains\n      only GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 — confirmed by their paper and repository. Public Mamba-2 hybrid\n      checkpoints exist but were trained with incompatible tokenizers or training setups, making\n      fair comparison impossible without retraining (out of GPU budget). Griffin checkpoints are\n      not publicly released. RWKV-6 hybrids don't exist in the literature. The n=3 limitation\n      is hard and cannot be resolved before the ARR deadline. This is a genuine limitation that\n      reviewers will flag, and the paper must address it directly. Mitigation: emphasize that\n      the three architectures represent three DISTINCT recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule,\n      linear attention + exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence), so the finding spans\n      mechanism diversity even if not architecture count.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Architecture expansion search results\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"Source\", \"Hybrid checkpoints available\", \"Compatible\", \"Reason if incompatible\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Mamba-2 hybrid\", \"Waleffe et al. 2024\", \"Yes (Zamba)\", \"No\", \"Different tokenizer, training data, no attention-ratio sweep\"]\n          - [\"Griffin\", \"De et al. 2024\", \"No\", \"N/A\", \"Checkpoints not public\"]\n          - [\"RWKV-6 hybrid\", \"N/A\", \"No\", \"N/A\", \"No hybrid variant exists\"]\n          - [\"Based (linear attention)\", \"Arora et al. 2024\", \"Partial\", \"No\", \"No systematic attention-ratio sweep\"]\n          - [\"Hawk/Griffin (RecurrentGemma)\", \"Google 2024\", \"Yes (2B)\", \"No\", \"Single scale, single config, no attention sweep\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Architecture expansion is impossible within constraints. The paper is locked at n=3. The\n      reframed narrative partially mitigates this: if the headline claim is \"per-example divergence\n      exists\" rather than \"convergence is universal,\" then n=3 is sufficient to demonstrate the\n      phenomenon. The generalization question (\"does this hold for all hybrid architectures?\")\n      becomes explicit future work. Honest framing: \"We demonstrate the phenomenon across three\n      architecturally distinct recurrence mechanisms; whether it extends to all hybrid architectures\n      is an open question requiring controlled training of additional architecture families.\"\n\n  - name: \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Deep analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at attn=8. Per-example comparison with 1.3B\n      at same attention ratio. Attempt to disentangle scale (340M vs 1.3B) from training tokens\n      (20B vs 100B) as explanatory variables for the rebound.\n    expected_results:\n      rebound_explained: false\n      scale_vs_tokens_disentangleable: false\n      per_example_overlap_340m_1_3b_jaccard: 0.12\n      rebound_samples_characterizable: false\n      rebound_unique_to_gdn: true\n      retnet_hgrn2_340m_trajectory: \"flat\"\n      training_tokens_340m: \"20B\"\n      training_tokens_1_3b: \"100B\"\n      tokens_per_param_340m: 58.8\n      tokens_per_param_1_3b: 76.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, the 340M rebound is unexplained and unexplainable with available data.\n      The fundamental confound is that 340M models were trained on 20B tokens while 1.3B models\n      saw 100B tokens — a 5x difference. Tokens-per-parameter ratio also differs (58.8 vs 76.9),\n      meaning 340M models are relatively undertrained. The rebound at high attention ratios could\n      be: (a) insufficient capacity at 340M to benefit from many attention layers, (b) undertrained\n      attention layers at 340M due to fewer tokens, (c) a genuine scale threshold effect. These\n      hypotheses are indistinguishable without training 340M models on 100B tokens or 1.3B models\n      on 20B tokens, neither of which is feasible. Per-example comparison shows low overlap between\n      340M and 1.3B rebound samples (Jaccard 0.12), and the rebound is most pronounced for GDN,\n      again raising the GDN-specific confound. RetNet and HGRN2 at 340M show essentially flat\n      trajectories with no clear rebound, making the \"rebound\" primarily a GDN phenomenon.\n      The scale story collapses into: \"we observe a difference between 340M and 1.3B but cannot\n      attribute it to scale, training compute, or their interaction.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"340M vs 1.3B trajectory comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"340M trajectory\", \"1.3B trajectory\", \"340M rebound at h=8\", \"Confound\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN\", \"Non-monotonic (rebound at h=8)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"Yes (std increases 40%)\", \"D004 confound + undertrained\"]\n          - [\"RetNet\", \"Flat (no clear trend)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"No\", \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"]\n          - [\"HGRN2\", \"Flat (slight improvement)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"No\", \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"]\n      - title: \"Training configuration confound\"\n        columns: [\"Scale\", \"Params\", \"Training tokens\", \"Tokens/param\", \"Chinchilla ratio\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"340M\", \"340M\", \"20B\", \"58.8\", \"Under-trained (~0.3x Chinchilla)\"]\n          - [\"1.3B\", \"1.3B\", \"100B\", \"76.9\", \"Under-trained (~0.4x Chinchilla)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-architecture std vs attention ratio, 340M and 1.3B overlaid. 1.3B shows clean monotonic decrease. 340M shows flat-to-noisy trajectory. GDN-specific rebound visible at h=8 for 340M but not for other architectures. Annotation marks the training token confound.\"\n      - \"Venn-style diagram of rebound samples at 340M (h=8 vs h=3) compared to 1.3B divergence samples. Overlap is minimal (Jaccard 0.12), suggesting different mechanisms at different scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Claim 2 (scale-dependent convergence) is effectively dead as a standalone claim. The binary\n      contrast is real — 340M doesn't converge, 1.3B does — but the attribution is hopelessly\n      confounded. With the worst tier reframe, this becomes a reported observation rather than a\n      claim: \"convergence was observed at 1.3B but not at 340M; however, these scales differ in\n      both parameter count and training compute, preventing causal attribution.\" In the reframed\n      paper focused on per-example divergence, scale becomes a secondary observation in a\n      limitations paragraph rather than a headline claim. The GDN-specificity of the rebound\n      further erodes confidence that it reflects a general scale phenomenon.\n\n  - name: \"Statistical robustness framework\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Bootstrap CIs for convergence metrics. Multi-comparison correction. Sensitivity analysis\n      with GDN excluded. Effect sizes.\n    expected_results:\n      bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_full:\n        point: 0.79\n        ci_lower: 0.61\n        ci_upper: 0.91\n      bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_gdn_excluded:\n        point: 0.18\n        ci_lower: -0.08\n        ci_upper: 0.41\n      gdn_excluded_rho: -0.60\n      gdn_excluded_p: 0.285\n      gdn_excluded_significant: false\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_trajectory: \"non-monotonic\"\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h0: 0.021\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h3: 0.019\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h6: 0.018\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h12: 0.020\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h24: 0.017\n      bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada: 0.048\n      bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada_6tests: 0.288\n      fdr_corrected_significant_benchmarks: 0\n      cohens_d_std_reduction_full: 1.82\n      cohens_d_std_reduction_gdn_excluded: 0.34\n    result_rationale: >\n      This is the core worst-tier finding. When GDN is excluded, the 79% std reduction collapses\n      to 18% — and the bootstrap 95% CI crosses zero ([-0.08, 0.41]). The RetNet-HGRN2 pair alone\n      shows a flat-to-slightly-decreasing std trajectory that is non-monotonic (slight increase at\n      h=12 before decreasing again at h=24). The Spearman ρ drops from -1.0 to -0.60 (p=0.285),\n      losing significance entirely. This means the convergence finding is substantially driven by\n      GDN's anomalous behavior — either the D004 confound or a GDN-specific architectural artifact.\n      Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks kills even LAMBADA's significance (0.048 × 6 = 0.288).\n      FDR correction yields zero significant benchmarks. The convergence claim, as originally stated,\n      does not survive robustness checks. Cohen's d drops from 1.82 (large, with GDN) to 0.34\n      (small, without GDN), confirming the effect is GDN-driven. The paper CANNOT lead with\n      convergence. The reframe to per-example divergence is not optional — it's necessary.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Sensitivity analysis: GDN inclusion/exclusion on LAMBADA convergence\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"All 3 architectures\", \"GDN excluded (RetNet + HGRN2 only)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Std reduction (%)\", \"79%\", \"18%\"]\n          - [\"Bootstrap 95% CI\", \"[0.61, 0.91]\", \"[-0.08, 0.41]\"]\n          - [\"Spearman ρ (std vs attn ratio)\", \"-1.00\", \"-0.60\"]\n          - [\"p-value (two-tailed)\", \"0.008\", \"0.285\"]\n          - [\"Cohen's d\", \"1.82\", \"0.34\"]\n          - [\"Monotonic decrease?\", \"Yes\", \"No (rebound at h=12)\"]\n      - title: \"Multiple comparison correction across benchmarks\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"Raw p\", \"Bonferroni p (×6)\", \"FDR-adjusted p\", \"Significant at α=0.05\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"0.008\", \"0.048\", \"0.048\", \"Borderline / No\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"0.117\", \"0.702\", \"0.351\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"MMLU\", \"0.117\", \"0.702\", \"0.351\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.742\", \"1.000\", \"0.891\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"0.883\", \"1.000\", \"0.883\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.608\", \"1.000\", \"0.891\", \"No\"]\n      - title: \"RetNet-HGRN2 cross-architecture std trajectory (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Attention layers\", \"RetNet acc\", \"HGRN2 acc\", \"Cross-arch std\", \"Δ from h=0\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"h=0\", \"0.412\", \"0.391\", \"0.0148\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"h=3\", \"0.428\", \"0.414\", \"0.0099\", \"-33%\"]\n          - [\"h=6\", \"0.441\", \"0.422\", \"0.0134\", \"-9%\"]\n          - [\"h=12\", \"0.455\", \"0.437\", \"0.0127\", \"-14%\"]\n          - [\"h=24\", \"0.468\", \"0.456\", \"0.0085\", \"-43%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bootstrap distribution of std reduction percentage, with and without GDN. Left panel (all 3 archs): distribution centered at 79%, well above zero. Right panel (GDN excluded): distribution centered at 18%, straddling zero. Dashed line at zero shows CI overlap.\"\n      - \"Cross-architecture std trajectory with GDN (solid, steep decline) and without GDN (dashed, noisy flat-to-slight decline). Shaded bootstrap CIs. Visual shows that convergence is dominated by GDN's trajectory pulling away from the other two.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the experiment that forces the reframe. The convergence finding does not survive the\n      most basic robustness check — removing one of three architectures. A reviewer who asks \"is this\n      just GDN being weird?\" can point to this table and say yes. The Bonferroni correction is also\n      damaging: even LAMBADA barely survives at 0.048, and a strict reviewer would reject it. The\n      paper must be transparent about this. In the reframed narrative, this analysis appears in\n      Section 4 as: \"We initially observed strong aggregate convergence, but robustness analysis\n      reveals this is substantially driven by a single architecture (GDN). We therefore focus on\n      the more robust finding: per-example divergence, which persists regardless of GDN's inclusion.\"\n      This honesty actually strengthens the paper — it shows rigorous self-evaluation.\n\n  - name: \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Plot aggregate convergence alongside per-example divergence metrics (Jaccard, net effect\n      asymmetry) as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Test whether convergence and divergence\n      co-evolve.\n    expected_results:\n      convergence_divergence_correlation_rho: -0.30\n      convergence_divergence_correlation_p: 0.45\n      jaccard_trend_with_attention: \"stable\"\n      jaccard_at_h0: null\n      jaccard_at_h3: 0.08\n      jaccard_at_h6: 0.07\n      jaccard_at_h12: 0.09\n      jaccard_at_h24: 0.07\n      net_effect_asymmetry_stable: true\n      paradox_visualization_compelling: \"moderate\"\n      co_evolution_significant: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, the convergence-divergence \"co-evolution\" is not significant because the\n      convergence side is broken (GDN-driven). Jaccard remains stable across attention ratios\n      (~0.07-0.09), meaning per-example divergence is always present regardless of how much\n      attention is added. This is actually consistent with the reframed narrative but kills the\n      \"paradox\" framing: there's no co-evolution because divergence is a constant, not a function\n      of attention ratio. The correlation between aggregate convergence (which is noisy without GDN)\n      and per-example Jaccard is weak (ρ=-0.30, p=0.45). The \"paradox\" becomes less of a paradox\n      and more of a simple observation: architectures always solve different examples (Jaccard ~0.08\n      is intrinsic), and sometimes their aggregate scores happen to converge (when GDN is included).\n      The visualization can still be striking — showing flat Jaccard against a converging (or not)\n      std line — but the causal \"more attention = more paradox\" story doesn't hold.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Convergence-divergence metrics by attention ratio (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Attention ratio (h/24)\", \"Cross-arch std (all 3)\", \"Cross-arch std (no GDN)\", \"Mean pairwise Jaccard\", \"Net effect asymmetry\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"0/24 (h=0)\", \"0.024\", \"0.015\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"3/24 (h=3)\", \"0.018\", \"0.010\", \"0.08\", \"0.42\"]\n          - [\"6/24 (h=6)\", \"0.012\", \"0.013\", \"0.07\", \"0.45\"]\n          - [\"12/24 (h=12)\", \"0.008\", \"0.013\", \"0.09\", \"0.39\"]\n          - [\"24/24 (h=24)\", \"0.005\", \"0.009\", \"0.07\", \"0.44\"]\n      - title: \"Convergence-divergence correlation\"\n        columns: [\"Metric pair\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"p-value\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Std (all 3) vs Jaccard\", \"-0.30\", \"0.45\", \"Not significant\"]\n          - [\"Std (no GDN) vs Jaccard\", \"-0.10\", \"0.82\", \"No relationship\"]\n          - [\"Std (all 3) vs net asymmetry\", \"0.10\", \"0.87\", \"No relationship\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis plot: left y-axis shows cross-architecture std (declining with attention for all-3, flat for no-GDN); right y-axis shows mean pairwise Jaccard (flat ~0.08 across all attention ratios). The visual 'paradox' is present when GDN is included but disappears without it. Both lines shown with and without GDN to make the point explicit.\"\n      - \"Per-example Sankey/alluvial diagram showing sample flow between helped/hurt categories across attention ratios for each architecture. Demonstrates that individual sample fates are stable — the same samples tend to be helped or hurt regardless of attention ratio, explaining the flat Jaccard.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The paradox framing survives only as a visual observation when GDN is included, not as a\n      statistically supported co-evolution. In the reframed paper, this becomes the key Figure 1:\n      \"Per-example divergence is architecture-intrinsic and attention-invariant.\" The flat Jaccard\n      line is actually the strongest visual argument for the reframed paper — it shows that no\n      amount of attention makes architectures agree on which examples to solve. This is more\n      powerful than a co-evolution story because it's simpler and more robust. The \"paradox\"\n      terminology should be softened to \"disconnect\" or \"dissociation\" — aggregate metrics move\n      while per-example identity doesn't. The Sankey diagram showing stable sample fates across\n      attention ratios could be a compelling supplementary figure.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11505,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11505},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a07c5373-374c-4780-b74d-58afa90838ad","uuid":"3e7d5c2f-8a56-48cb-a2bd-1352891bcc96"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":190300,"duration_api_ms":191653,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Abandon convergence as headline. Reframe paper around the robust finding that survives all checks:\n  per-example divergence (Jaccard <0.10) is architecture-intrinsic and persists regardless of aggregate\n  convergence trends. New title direction: 'Aggregate Metrics Hide Architecture-Specific Behavior in\n  Hybrid Language Models.' Lead claim becomes: hybrid architectures that look identical on benchmarks\n  are solving fundamentally different subsets of examples — standard evaluation is blind to this.\n  Convergence (when it exists) is demoted to a secondary observation. Practical implication reframed:\n  architecture selection matters even when leaderboard scores converge, because failure modes don't\n  overlap → ensemble/routing potential. Trade-off: weaker theoretical story, but more defensible claims.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Re-run lm-eval with --log_samples on 1.3B models for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge.\n      Collect per-example correctness vectors across architectures and attention ratios.\n      Compute pairwise Jaccard on helped/hurt sets; test whether per-example convergence\n      is absent on non-LAMBADA benchmarks.\n    expected_results:\n      hellaswag_jaccard_mean: 0.08\n      hellaswag_jaccard_std: 0.03\n      arc_easy_jaccard_mean: 0.11\n      arc_challenge_jaccard_mean: 0.13\n      hellaswag_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.15\n      hellaswag_per_example_convergence_p: 0.62\n      arc_easy_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.40\n      arc_easy_per_example_convergence_p: 0.35\n      winogrande_per_example_convergence_rho: -0.20\n      winogrande_per_example_convergence_p: 0.55\n      n_benchmarks_with_per_example_convergence: 0\n      arc_easy_aggregate_convergence_suggestive: true\n      arc_easy_per_example_mechanism_matches_lambada: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, per-example analysis on HellaSwag and WinoGrande reveals NO convergence\n      signal at sample level either — confirming task-specificity holds. However, ARC-Easy shows\n      a weak but non-trivial per-example signal (rho=-0.40, p=0.35) that partially mirrors LAMBADA's\n      pattern, threatening the clean \"retrieval-specific\" story. The effect is too weak to be\n      significant but too present to ignore, collapsing the task-specificity claim into a muddier\n      \"convergence is generic but weak everywhere except LAMBADA where GDN amplifies it\" narrative.\n      Jaccard values on HellaSwag (~0.08) confirm per-example divergence generalizes across benchmarks\n      — this is the one robust finding. ARC tasks show slightly higher Jaccard (0.11-0.13), suggesting\n      reasoning tasks have marginally more overlap in architecture behavior but still fundamentally\n      low. The critical implication: convergence is NOT cleanly retrieval-specific (undermining Claim 1's\n      dose-response framing), but per-example divergence IS universal (supporting the reframed narrative).\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"Aggregate ρ (std vs attn)\", \"Per-example ρ\", \"Per-example p\", \"Mean Jaccard\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"-1.00\", \"-0.90\", \"0.017\", \"0.07\", \"Convergent (but GDN-driven)\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"-0.70\", \"-0.40\", \"0.350\", \"0.11\", \"Weak signal, non-significant\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"-0.30\", \"-0.10\", \"0.780\", \"0.13\", \"No convergence\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.30\", \"-0.15\", \"0.620\", \"0.08\", \"No convergence\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.10\", \"-0.20\", \"0.550\", \"0.09\", \"No convergence\"]\n      - title: \"Pairwise Jaccard similarity on helped sets (1.3B, h3-1 config)\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"GDN-RetNet\", \"GDN-HGRN2\", \"RetNet-HGRN2\", \"Mean\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"0.06\", \"0.08\", \"0.07\", \"0.07\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"0.10\", \"0.12\", \"0.11\", \"0.11\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"0.11\", \"0.14\", \"0.13\", \"0.13\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.07\", \"0.09\", \"0.08\", \"0.08\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.08\", \"0.10\", \"0.09\", \"0.09\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap of Jaccard similarity across all benchmark × architecture pairs. Uniformly cold (blue) across all benchmarks, demonstrating that per-example divergence is benchmark-agnostic. LAMBADA and HellaSwag nearly identical in Jaccard despite opposite aggregate convergence trends.\"\n      - \"Scatter: aggregate convergence ρ vs per-example convergence ρ per benchmark. No clean linear relationship; ARC-Easy is an outlier with moderate per-example signal despite aggregate suggestiveness, breaking the expected dose-response pattern.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ARC-Easy per-example signal (ρ=-0.40) is the worst-case complication. It's not significant\n      (p=0.35) but it's not zero either. A reviewer can argue: \"convergence exists weakly everywhere,\n      LAMBADA just happens to cross significance because GDN's anomalous behavior inflates the signal.\"\n      This undermines the retrieval-specificity framing. However, the universal low Jaccard (all <0.15)\n      is the paper's salvation — it doesn't depend on convergence at all. The reframe: \"regardless of\n      whether architectures converge in aggregate, they solve different examples\" is supported by every\n      benchmark. Dose-response narrative for convergence strength is dead; replace with: \"per-example\n      divergence is the invariant, convergence is the variable.\"\n\n  - name: \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Extend Phase 1 surface feature analysis with deeper features: (a) perplexity of target token\n      under pure recurrent baseline (no attention), (b) topic/domain classification of LAMBADA\n      contexts using a lightweight classifier or embedding clustering. Test whether these features\n      differentiate helped vs hurt samples within and across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      perplexity_gdn_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.18\n      perplexity_hgrn2_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.09\n      perplexity_retnet_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.31\n      perplexity_cross_arch_interaction_p: 0.42\n      topic_gdn_chi2_p: 0.35\n      topic_hgrn2_chi2_p: 0.22\n      topic_retnet_chi2_p: 0.47\n      topic_cross_arch_difference_p: 0.61\n      any_feature_significant_p_lt_01: false\n      n_features_marginal_p_lt_05: 1\n      best_single_feature_auc: 0.54\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, ALL Phase 2 features are non-significant. Perplexity under baseline\n      shows a marginal trend for HGRN2 (p=0.09) — helped samples tend to have slightly higher\n      baseline perplexity, suggesting attention helps on \"harder\" items — but the effect doesn't\n      replicate across GDN (p=0.18) or RetNet (p=0.31), and the cross-architecture interaction\n      is nowhere near significant (p=0.42). Topic/domain analysis reveals no systematic pattern\n      at all (all p>0.2). The best single-feature AUC for predicting helped/hurt is 0.54,\n      barely above chance. This means we have ZERO mechanistic toehold: we cannot explain what\n      makes a sample helped vs hurt by any measurable property. The architecture-specific effect\n      is real (Jaccard proves it) but opaque. This is the worst case for Concern C — no causal\n      mechanism is articulable. The reframe must embrace this opacity: \"the effect is detectable\n      but not yet explainable, which itself is a finding about the limits of current interpretability.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Phase 2 feature analysis: helped vs hurt samples (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\"\n        columns: [\"Feature\", \"GDN p-value\", \"HGRN2 p-value\", \"RetNet p-value\", \"Cross-arch interaction p\", \"Best AUC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Baseline perplexity\", \"0.18\", \"0.09\", \"0.31\", \"0.42\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"Topic (fiction/non-fiction)\", \"0.35\", \"0.22\", \"0.47\", \"0.61\", \"0.51\"]\n          - [\"Topic (10-cluster)\", \"0.41\", \"0.29\", \"0.53\", \"0.58\", \"0.52\"]\n          - [\"Token frequency (Phase 1)\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \">0.5\", \"0.51\"]\n          - [\"Context length (Phase 1)\", \">0.2\", \">0.2\", \"0.012\", \">0.5\", \"0.52\"]\n      - title: \"Combined feature model (logistic regression, helped vs hurt)\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"All features AUC\", \"95% CI\", \"Permutation baseline AUC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN\", \"0.53\", \"[0.49, 0.57]\", \"0.50\"]\n          - [\"HGRN2\", \"0.55\", \"[0.51, 0.59]\", \"0.50\"]\n          - [\"RetNet\", \"0.52\", \"[0.48, 0.56]\", \"0.50\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Violin plots of baseline perplexity for helped vs hurt samples, split by architecture. Distributions almost entirely overlapping. HGRN2 shows a slight rightward shift for helped samples but the overlap is >90%.\"\n      - \"ROC curves for combined feature model per architecture. All curves hug the diagonal, with AUCs in [0.52, 0.55]. Visual confirmation that no accessible feature set predicts helped/hurt status.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Complete mechanistic failure. Six features tested across two phases, none reaches significance\n      with cross-architecture consistency. The HGRN2 perplexity trend (p=0.09) is tantalizing but\n      unreliable — it doesn't survive multiple comparison correction and doesn't generalize to the\n      other architectures. The combined model AUC of 0.53-0.55 means that even throwing all features\n      together, we predict helped/hurt status barely better than a coin flip. This is simultaneously\n      the worst and most interesting outcome: the architecture-specific effect is NOT driven by\n      any obvious data property. For the reframed paper, this becomes: \"standard sample features\n      cannot explain which examples each architecture handles differently, suggesting the divergence\n      operates at a representational level not captured by surface or moderate-depth features.\"\n\n  - name: \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Extract attention weight matrices from h3-1 hybrid models for all 3 architectures on LAMBADA\n      samples. Analyze: position-level attention entropy, correlation between attention focus and\n      helped/hurt identity, cross-architecture attention pattern similarity (CKA or cosine).\n    expected_results:\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_gdn: 0.07\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_hgrn2: 0.12\n      attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_retnet: 0.04\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_retnet: 0.31\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_hgrn2: 0.28\n      cross_arch_attention_cka_retnet_hgrn2: 0.35\n      attention_focus_position_correlation_with_effect: 0.09\n      attention_pattern_predicts_helped_auc: 0.56\n      interpretable_pattern_found: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, attention patterns show low correlation with helped/hurt identity (r<0.15\n      for all architectures). Attention entropy — a measure of how focused vs diffuse the attention\n      is — differs only trivially between helped and hurt samples. Cross-architecture CKA similarity\n      is moderate (~0.30), meaning attention layers are doing somewhat similar things structurally\n      across architectures, but this structural similarity does NOT predict the divergent per-example\n      outcomes. The attention pattern AUC for predicting helped/hurt (0.56) is only marginally\n      better than the feature-based model and far from interpretable. No clear \"smoking gun\" pattern\n      emerges (e.g., no finding like \"GDN attends to nearby context while RetNet attends to distant\n      context on helped samples\"). This makes Concern C (no causal mechanism) essentially unanswerable\n      within the scope of this paper. The attention layer is doing something architecture-specific,\n      but what it's doing doesn't cleanly map onto which examples benefit.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Attention pattern analysis (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"GDN\", \"HGRN2\", \"RetNet\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Mean attention entropy (helped)\", \"2.31\", \"2.45\", \"2.28\"]\n          - [\"Mean attention entropy (hurt)\", \"2.34\", \"2.39\", \"2.30\"]\n          - [\"Entropy correlation with effect size\", \"0.07\", \"0.12\", \"0.04\"]\n          - [\"% attention on last 10 tokens (helped)\", \"0.34\", \"0.29\", \"0.36\"]\n          - [\"% attention on last 10 tokens (hurt)\", \"0.33\", \"0.30\", \"0.35\"]\n          - [\"Attention predicts helped AUC\", \"0.54\", \"0.57\", \"0.53\"]\n      - title: \"Cross-architecture attention similarity (CKA, h3-1 layer)\"\n        columns: [\"Pair\", \"CKA (all samples)\", \"CKA (helped subset)\", \"CKA (hurt subset)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN–RetNet\", \"0.31\", \"0.29\", \"0.33\"]\n          - [\"GDN–HGRN2\", \"0.28\", \"0.26\", \"0.30\"]\n          - [\"RetNet–HGRN2\", \"0.35\", \"0.33\", \"0.37\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Attention heatmaps for 3 representative LAMBADA examples (one helped by all, one hurt by all, one divergent). No visually distinguishable pattern difference. Attention distributions look qualitatively similar across architectures even on divergent samples.\"\n      - \"Scatter plot: per-sample attention entropy vs effect size (log-prob change), colored by architecture. Cloud is roughly circular with no discernible trend. Correlation lines nearly flat (all |r|<0.15).\"\n    analysis: >\n      Attention analysis provides no mechanistic explanation. The moderate CKA values (~0.30) are\n      actually a nuisance finding — attention is somewhat similar across architectures, which means\n      the divergent per-example outcomes must arise from the INTERACTION between attention and the\n      different recurrence mechanisms, not from attention alone. This is plausible but untestable\n      within our experimental scope — we'd need to ablate the recurrence mechanism while holding\n      attention fixed, which requires retraining. For the reframed paper, this section becomes:\n      \"attention patterns alone do not explain architecture-specific effects, suggesting the mechanism\n      operates through attention-recurrence interaction\" — a honest null result that points to\n      future work. The figures can still appear in the paper as evidence of thoroughness.\n\n  - name: \"Architecture expansion check\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Search Wang et al. model zoo and public repositories for additional hybrid architectures\n      (Mamba-2 hybrids, RWKV hybrids, Griffin, etc.) compatible with lm-eval. If found, evaluate\n      on LAMBADA to test convergence generalization.\n    expected_results:\n      additional_architectures_found: 0\n      additional_architectures_compatible: 0\n      n_architectures_tested: 3\n      wang_et_al_only_gdn_retnet_hgrn2: true\n      public_hybrid_checkpoints_available: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, no additional architectures are available. Wang et al.'s model zoo contains\n      only GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 — confirmed by their paper and repository. Public Mamba-2 hybrid\n      checkpoints exist but were trained with incompatible tokenizers or training setups, making\n      fair comparison impossible without retraining (out of GPU budget). Griffin checkpoints are\n      not publicly released. RWKV-6 hybrids don't exist in the literature. The n=3 limitation\n      is hard and cannot be resolved before the ARR deadline. This is a genuine limitation that\n      reviewers will flag, and the paper must address it directly. Mitigation: emphasize that\n      the three architectures represent three DISTINCT recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule,\n      linear attention + exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence), so the finding spans\n      mechanism diversity even if not architecture count.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Architecture expansion search results\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"Source\", \"Hybrid checkpoints available\", \"Compatible\", \"Reason if incompatible\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Mamba-2 hybrid\", \"Waleffe et al. 2024\", \"Yes (Zamba)\", \"No\", \"Different tokenizer, training data, no attention-ratio sweep\"]\n          - [\"Griffin\", \"De et al. 2024\", \"No\", \"N/A\", \"Checkpoints not public\"]\n          - [\"RWKV-6 hybrid\", \"N/A\", \"No\", \"N/A\", \"No hybrid variant exists\"]\n          - [\"Based (linear attention)\", \"Arora et al. 2024\", \"Partial\", \"No\", \"No systematic attention-ratio sweep\"]\n          - [\"Hawk/Griffin (RecurrentGemma)\", \"Google 2024\", \"Yes (2B)\", \"No\", \"Single scale, single config, no attention sweep\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Architecture expansion is impossible within constraints. The paper is locked at n=3. The\n      reframed narrative partially mitigates this: if the headline claim is \"per-example divergence\n      exists\" rather than \"convergence is universal,\" then n=3 is sufficient to demonstrate the\n      phenomenon. The generalization question (\"does this hold for all hybrid architectures?\")\n      becomes explicit future work. Honest framing: \"We demonstrate the phenomenon across three\n      architecturally distinct recurrence mechanisms; whether it extends to all hybrid architectures\n      is an open question requiring controlled training of additional architecture families.\"\n\n  - name: \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Deep analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at attn=8. Per-example comparison with 1.3B\n      at same attention ratio. Attempt to disentangle scale (340M vs 1.3B) from training tokens\n      (20B vs 100B) as explanatory variables for the rebound.\n    expected_results:\n      rebound_explained: false\n      scale_vs_tokens_disentangleable: false\n      per_example_overlap_340m_1_3b_jaccard: 0.12\n      rebound_samples_characterizable: false\n      rebound_unique_to_gdn: true\n      retnet_hgrn2_340m_trajectory: \"flat\"\n      training_tokens_340m: \"20B\"\n      training_tokens_1_3b: \"100B\"\n      tokens_per_param_340m: 58.8\n      tokens_per_param_1_3b: 76.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, the 340M rebound is unexplained and unexplainable with available data.\n      The fundamental confound is that 340M models were trained on 20B tokens while 1.3B models\n      saw 100B tokens — a 5x difference. Tokens-per-parameter ratio also differs (58.8 vs 76.9),\n      meaning 340M models are relatively undertrained. The rebound at high attention ratios could\n      be: (a) insufficient capacity at 340M to benefit from many attention layers, (b) undertrained\n      attention layers at 340M due to fewer tokens, (c) a genuine scale threshold effect. These\n      hypotheses are indistinguishable without training 340M models on 100B tokens or 1.3B models\n      on 20B tokens, neither of which is feasible. Per-example comparison shows low overlap between\n      340M and 1.3B rebound samples (Jaccard 0.12), and the rebound is most pronounced for GDN,\n      again raising the GDN-specific confound. RetNet and HGRN2 at 340M show essentially flat\n      trajectories with no clear rebound, making the \"rebound\" primarily a GDN phenomenon.\n      The scale story collapses into: \"we observe a difference between 340M and 1.3B but cannot\n      attribute it to scale, training compute, or their interaction.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"340M vs 1.3B trajectory comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"340M trajectory\", \"1.3B trajectory\", \"340M rebound at h=8\", \"Confound\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"GDN\", \"Non-monotonic (rebound at h=8)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"Yes (std increases 40%)\", \"D004 confound + undertrained\"]\n          - [\"RetNet\", \"Flat (no clear trend)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"No\", \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"]\n          - [\"HGRN2\", \"Flat (slight improvement)\", \"Monotonic convergence\", \"No\", \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"]\n      - title: \"Training configuration confound\"\n        columns: [\"Scale\", \"Params\", \"Training tokens\", \"Tokens/param\", \"Chinchilla ratio\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"340M\", \"340M\", \"20B\", \"58.8\", \"Under-trained (~0.3x Chinchilla)\"]\n          - [\"1.3B\", \"1.3B\", \"100B\", \"76.9\", \"Under-trained (~0.4x Chinchilla)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-architecture std vs attention ratio, 340M and 1.3B overlaid. 1.3B shows clean monotonic decrease. 340M shows flat-to-noisy trajectory. GDN-specific rebound visible at h=8 for 340M but not for other architectures. Annotation marks the training token confound.\"\n      - \"Venn-style diagram of rebound samples at 340M (h=8 vs h=3) compared to 1.3B divergence samples. Overlap is minimal (Jaccard 0.12), suggesting different mechanisms at different scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Claim 2 (scale-dependent convergence) is effectively dead as a standalone claim. The binary\n      contrast is real — 340M doesn't converge, 1.3B does — but the attribution is hopelessly\n      confounded. With the worst tier reframe, this becomes a reported observation rather than a\n      claim: \"convergence was observed at 1.3B but not at 340M; however, these scales differ in\n      both parameter count and training compute, preventing causal attribution.\" In the reframed\n      paper focused on per-example divergence, scale becomes a secondary observation in a\n      limitations paragraph rather than a headline claim. The GDN-specificity of the rebound\n      further erodes confidence that it reflects a general scale phenomenon.\n\n  - name: \"Statistical robustness framework\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Bootstrap CIs for convergence metrics. Multi-comparison correction. Sensitivity analysis\n      with GDN excluded. Effect sizes.\n    expected_results:\n      bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_full:\n        point: 0.79\n        ci_lower: 0.61\n        ci_upper: 0.91\n      bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_gdn_excluded:\n        point: 0.18\n        ci_lower: -0.08\n        ci_upper: 0.41\n      gdn_excluded_rho: -0.60\n      gdn_excluded_p: 0.285\n      gdn_excluded_significant: false\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_trajectory: \"non-monotonic\"\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h0: 0.021\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h3: 0.019\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h6: 0.018\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h12: 0.020\n      retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h24: 0.017\n      bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada: 0.048\n      bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada_6tests: 0.288\n      fdr_corrected_significant_benchmarks: 0\n      cohens_d_std_reduction_full: 1.82\n      cohens_d_std_reduction_gdn_excluded: 0.34\n    result_rationale: >\n      This is the core worst-tier finding. When GDN is excluded, the 79% std reduction collapses\n      to 18% — and the bootstrap 95% CI crosses zero ([-0.08, 0.41]). The RetNet-HGRN2 pair alone\n      shows a flat-to-slightly-decreasing std trajectory that is non-monotonic (slight increase at\n      h=12 before decreasing again at h=24). The Spearman ρ drops from -1.0 to -0.60 (p=0.285),\n      losing significance entirely. This means the convergence finding is substantially driven by\n      GDN's anomalous behavior — either the D004 confound or a GDN-specific architectural artifact.\n      Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks kills even LAMBADA's significance (0.048 × 6 = 0.288).\n      FDR correction yields zero significant benchmarks. The convergence claim, as originally stated,\n      does not survive robustness checks. Cohen's d drops from 1.82 (large, with GDN) to 0.34\n      (small, without GDN), confirming the effect is GDN-driven. The paper CANNOT lead with\n      convergence. The reframe to per-example divergence is not optional — it's necessary.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Sensitivity analysis: GDN inclusion/exclusion on LAMBADA convergence\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"All 3 architectures\", \"GDN excluded (RetNet + HGRN2 only)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Std reduction (%)\", \"79%\", \"18%\"]\n          - [\"Bootstrap 95% CI\", \"[0.61, 0.91]\", \"[-0.08, 0.41]\"]\n          - [\"Spearman ρ (std vs attn ratio)\", \"-1.00\", \"-0.60\"]\n          - [\"p-value (two-tailed)\", \"0.008\", \"0.285\"]\n          - [\"Cohen's d\", \"1.82\", \"0.34\"]\n          - [\"Monotonic decrease?\", \"Yes\", \"No (rebound at h=12)\"]\n      - title: \"Multiple comparison correction across benchmarks\"\n        columns: [\"Benchmark\", \"Raw p\", \"Bonferroni p (×6)\", \"FDR-adjusted p\", \"Significant at α=0.05\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LAMBADA\", \"0.008\", \"0.048\", \"0.048\", \"Borderline / No\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Easy\", \"0.117\", \"0.702\", \"0.351\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"MMLU\", \"0.117\", \"0.702\", \"0.351\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"HellaSwag\", \"0.742\", \"1.000\", \"0.891\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"ARC-Challenge\", \"0.883\", \"1.000\", \"0.883\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"WinoGrande\", \"0.608\", \"1.000\", \"0.891\", \"No\"]\n      - title: \"RetNet-HGRN2 cross-architecture std trajectory (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Attention layers\", \"RetNet acc\", \"HGRN2 acc\", \"Cross-arch std\", \"Δ from h=0\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"h=0\", \"0.412\", \"0.391\", \"0.0148\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"h=3\", \"0.428\", \"0.414\", \"0.0099\", \"-33%\"]\n          - [\"h=6\", \"0.441\", \"0.422\", \"0.0134\", \"-9%\"]\n          - [\"h=12\", \"0.455\", \"0.437\", \"0.0127\", \"-14%\"]\n          - [\"h=24\", \"0.468\", \"0.456\", \"0.0085\", \"-43%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bootstrap distribution of std reduction percentage, with and without GDN. Left panel (all 3 archs): distribution centered at 79%, well above zero. Right panel (GDN excluded): distribution centered at 18%, straddling zero. Dashed line at zero shows CI overlap.\"\n      - \"Cross-architecture std trajectory with GDN (solid, steep decline) and without GDN (dashed, noisy flat-to-slight decline). Shaded bootstrap CIs. Visual shows that convergence is dominated by GDN's trajectory pulling away from the other two.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the experiment that forces the reframe. The convergence finding does not survive the\n      most basic robustness check — removing one of three architectures. A reviewer who asks \"is this\n      just GDN being weird?\" can point to this table and say yes. The Bonferroni correction is also\n      damaging: even LAMBADA barely survives at 0.048, and a strict reviewer would reject it. The\n      paper must be transparent about this. In the reframed narrative, this analysis appears in\n      Section 4 as: \"We initially observed strong aggregate convergence, but robustness analysis\n      reveals this is substantially driven by a single architecture (GDN). We therefore focus on\n      the more robust finding: per-example divergence, which persists regardless of GDN's inclusion.\"\n      This honesty actually strengthens the paper — it shows rigorous self-evaluation.\n\n  - name: \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Plot aggregate convergence alongside per-example divergence metrics (Jaccard, net effect\n      asymmetry) as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Test whether convergence and divergence\n      co-evolve.\n    expected_results:\n      convergence_divergence_correlation_rho: -0.30\n      convergence_divergence_correlation_p: 0.45\n      jaccard_trend_with_attention: \"stable\"\n      jaccard_at_h0: null\n      jaccard_at_h3: 0.08\n      jaccard_at_h6: 0.07\n      jaccard_at_h12: 0.09\n      jaccard_at_h24: 0.07\n      net_effect_asymmetry_stable: true\n      paradox_visualization_compelling: \"moderate\"\n      co_evolution_significant: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the worst tier, the convergence-divergence \"co-evolution\" is not significant because the\n      convergence side is broken (GDN-driven). Jaccard remains stable across attention ratios\n      (~0.07-0.09), meaning per-example divergence is always present regardless of how much\n      attention is added. This is actually consistent with the reframed narrative but kills the\n      \"paradox\" framing: there's no co-evolution because divergence is a constant, not a function\n      of attention ratio. The correlation between aggregate convergence (which is noisy without GDN)\n      and per-example Jaccard is weak (ρ=-0.30, p=0.45). The \"paradox\" becomes less of a paradox\n      and more of a simple observation: architectures always solve different examples (Jaccard ~0.08\n      is intrinsic), and sometimes their aggregate scores happen to converge (when GDN is included).\n      The visualization can still be striking — showing flat Jaccard against a converging (or not)\n      std line — but the causal \"more attention = more paradox\" story doesn't hold.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Convergence-divergence metrics by attention ratio (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\"\n        columns: [\"Attention ratio (h/24)\", \"Cross-arch std (all 3)\", \"Cross-arch std (no GDN)\", \"Mean pairwise Jaccard\", \"Net effect asymmetry\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"0/24 (h=0)\", \"0.024\", \"0.015\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"3/24 (h=3)\", \"0.018\", \"0.010\", \"0.08\", \"0.42\"]\n          - [\"6/24 (h=6)\", \"0.012\", \"0.013\", \"0.07\", \"0.45\"]\n          - [\"12/24 (h=12)\", \"0.008\", \"0.013\", \"0.09\", \"0.39\"]\n          - [\"24/24 (h=24)\", \"0.005\", \"0.009\", \"0.07\", \"0.44\"]\n      - title: \"Convergence-divergence correlation\"\n        columns: [\"Metric pair\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"p-value\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Std (all 3) vs Jaccard\", \"-0.30\", \"0.45\", \"Not significant\"]\n          - [\"Std (no GDN) vs Jaccard\", \"-0.10\", \"0.82\", \"No relationship\"]\n          - [\"Std (all 3) vs net asymmetry\", \"0.10\", \"0.87\", \"No relationship\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis plot: left y-axis shows cross-architecture std (declining with attention for all-3, flat for no-GDN); right y-axis shows mean pairwise Jaccard (flat ~0.08 across all attention ratios). The visual 'paradox' is present when GDN is included but disappears without it. Both lines shown with and without GDN to make the point explicit.\"\n      - \"Per-example Sankey/alluvial diagram showing sample flow between helped/hurt categories across attention ratios for each architecture. Demonstrates that individual sample fates are stable — the same samples tend to be helped or hurt regardless of attention ratio, explaining the flat Jaccard.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The paradox framing survives only as a visual observation when GDN is included, not as a\n      statistically supported co-evolution. In the reframed paper, this becomes the key Figure 1:\n      \"Per-example divergence is architecture-intrinsic and attention-invariant.\" The flat Jaccard\n      line is actually the strongest visual argument for the reframed paper — it shows that no\n      amount of attention makes architectures agree on which examples to solve. This is more\n      powerful than a co-evolution story because it's simpler and more robust. The \"paradox\"\n      terminology should be softened to \"disconnect\" or \"dissociation\" — aggregate metrics move\n      while per-example identity doesn't. The Sankey diagram showing stable sample fates across\n      attention ratios could be a compelling supplementary figure.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a07c5373-374c-4780-b74d-58afa90838ad","total_cost_usd":0.32634175,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11505,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":9678,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11505,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9678,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11505},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6465,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.006555,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":9678,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11505,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.31978675,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"1c66b6a1-d16b-453c-b3cf-9ab7402f604a"}
